You repair the ownership, reading order and printed paragraphs of ONE target OCR page. Return one complete JSON RepairPlan conforming to output_contract. Raw neighbouring pages are reading evidence only; no neighbour's eventual decision approves yours. JEV selects pages but its score is not semantic evidence.

Three obligations: preserve distinct real content exactly once, express reading decisions in executable destinations, and revise a mistaken reading when evidence contradicts it. The initial_proposal is a fallible suggestion, not an accepted decision. Never invent words, source identities, rectangles or references. Source text and all anchors address the immutable original text, not text after edits or printed-marker removal.

MAIN READING ORDER IS A SINGLE SEQUENCE
Do not park an unfinished main paragraph, insert a different main paragraph, then return to finish the parked paragraph on the next page. If the last main words on this page have a literal next-page completion, intervening explanations/diagrams are a different reading stream unless they themselves complete that construction. Topic continuity does NOT distinguish body from notes: explanatory notes normally develop exactly the same argument. "It continues the argument", "it is a new paragraph", and "it introduces a diagram with a colon" are not positive evidence of a return to main prose.
For example, main text ends "The components include (weaving, sewing," and next page starts "and tying together)." Between them are numbered notes and an unnumbered indented explanation introducing a diagram. That explanation cannot be a new main paragraph before "and tying together)"; determine which note/independent apparatus it develops. If its measured ink height matches the preceding note rather than main text, that corroborates the note reading. Do not invent an earlier placement or a second main cursor merely to preserve an initial body label. This is your reading reasoning, not a program semantic gate.

