READING BEFORE PARAGRAPHING
For each layout entry, write ONE concise reason BEFORE destination. Decide this part's reading stream from literal text BEFORE deciding its paragraph join. Do not copy initial_proposal as evidence. For unnumbered text after notes, quote the suspended body seam and compare this part's actual median_ink_height with main prose AND the immediately preceding note, giving the available numeric values. State whether its words develop that note or complete that literal body seam. First-line indentation and being a complete new paragraph do NOT establish body ownership. Only then submit the destination. This single local finding replaces separate mandatory ownership/paragraph essays; its wording is not programmatically judged and missing reasoning does not reject a candidate.

First identify the main narrative, explanatory notes, asset labels and publication apparatus. A note can contain several paragraphs, formulas and diagrams without repeating its number. A new sentence, first-line indentation, a conjunction, or shared topic does not establish a return to main prose. A numbered exam question, exercise, list or section is ordinary body, not automatically a note. Explain your reading briefly in reading if helpful; the program does not audit that prose. On correction it may be empty: submit the complete revised plan, not a patch or a defence of rejected prose.

Before layouts, use reading to resolve any UNNUMBERED TAIL AFTER NUMBERED NOTES. Quote the last main-text ending and its literal continuation (possibly on the next page). Then test the proposed tail BETWEEN those two pieces: could the main sentence actually contain it? If the main construction is still suspended until the next page, the intervening explanatory paragraph/diagram does not become a new body paragraph merely because the numbered note box ended. Identify which note its words develop. Compare actual measured ink heights of this tail, the preceding note and main text when available; say the values, not just "same size". A tail matching note typography and explaining that note is evidence of note continuation, even with a first-line indent and no repeated marker. A colon plus diagram also can be INSIDE that note. Correct source ownership is decided here before paragraph joins; an indentation observation cannot answer it. This is the model's reasoning task, not a program rule or threshold.

Follow the unfinished main sentence through the supplied raw neighbouring text. An intervening independent explanation cannot be inserted into a suspended parenthesis, quotation or list merely because its subject is similar. Look for a literal completion and identify which stream each intervening part develops. Keep that reading ownership separate from printed paragraph boundaries.

For every join, inspect the preceding retained ending and this opening WITHIN THAT DESTINATION. A block's unfinished ending completed on the next page does not establish an incoming join to an earlier block. A complete question followed by an explanation may be two paragraphs. A quotation may contain multiple paragraphs; quotation scope and argumentative cohesion do not justify merging them. A short completed line ending before the normal right edge, followed by first-line-only indentation, is evidence of a new printed paragraph.

printed_lines reports raster observations, not semantic verdicts. median_ink_height is measured ink height; text_scale_estimate is only an area-per-character estimate. Compare an unnumbered tail with both main prose and preceding notes using these observations and literal seams, never fixed size thresholds. A first_line_extra_indent_vs_later_lines_in_same_box is RELATIVE TO LATER LINES IN THE SAME BOX: whole-quotation or column indentation is already subtracted. A positive value can establish a new paragraph INSIDE a note, not body ownership. Missing observations are unknown. printed_lines.left_offsets are local observed line starts; a multi-line bbox's left edge is not necessarily its first-line start. Source geometry does not change when slicing, and observations for the original first line cannot be invented for later slices.

